  "theory": {
    "intro": "The logarithm laws come straight from the index laws, because a logarithm is an index. Adding two logs matches multiplying, subtracting matches dividing, and a number written in front of a log becomes a power inside it. The same three laws run both ways: you can **combine** several logs of the same base into one, or **expand** one log into a sum, difference or multiple. Every answer here stays written as a logarithm — you are rearranging logs, not evaluating them.",
    "facts": [
      "**Product law:** $\\log_{a}(mn)=\\log_{a}m+\\log_{a}n$ — a sum of logs combines into the log of a product.",
      "**Quotient law:** $\\log_{a}\\!\\left(\\frac{m}{n}\\right)=\\log_{a}m-\\log_{a}n$ — a difference of logs combines into the log of a quotient.",
      "**Power law:** $\\log_{a}(m^{n})=n\\log_{a}m$ — a coefficient in front of a log is the same as an index on its argument.",
      "The product and quotient laws only apply when every logarithm has the **same base**.",
      "The laws work both directions: read left-to-right to **expand** one log into several, or right-to-left to **combine** several logs into one.",
      "A coefficient and an index are interchangeable, so $3\\log_{a}2=\\log_{a}2^{3}=\\log_{a}8$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Check that every logarithm being combined has the same base.",
      "Turn any coefficient into an index, then products add and quotients subtract the arguments.",
      "Write the result as a single logarithm when combining, or as separate log terms when expanding."
    ]
  },
